## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical security design for dynamic plugins in multi-tenant edge environments, where trust and isolation are critical. It combines supply chain attestations, WASM sandboxing, per-tenant isolation, and incident response, mirroring real-world constraints at Coinbase/Netflix/Cloudflare.

## Key Concepts
- WASM sandboxing and host-call controls
- SBOM, Sigstore/cosign attestations
- Tenant isolation and revocation mechanisms
- Tamper-evident logging + forensics
- CI/CD gates and runtime monitors

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: verify plugin attestation before load
function verifyPlugin(attestation, sbom, rootKey) {
  if (!verifySignature(attestation.signature, rootKey)) return false;
  if (!verifySBOM(sbom)) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate supply-chain integrity under frequent dependency updates?
- How would you measure the performance impact of per-plugin isolation?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates understanding of practical CORS security fixes, including origin whitelisting, credentials handling, and preflight validation in production applications.

## Key Concepts

- CORS and the ACAO header behavior with wildcards
- Access-Control-Allow-Credentials and the Vary: Origin header
- Preflight OPTIONS request handling
- Authentication boundaries and data exposure risks

## Code Example

```javascript
// Express middleware
const ALLOWED_ORIGIN = 'https://app.example.com';

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const origin = req.headers.origin;

  if (origin === ALLOWED_ORIGIN) {
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', ALLOWED_ORIGIN);
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Credentials', 'true');
    res.setHeader('Vary', 'Origin');
  }

  if (req.method === 'OPTIONS') {
    return res.send(204);
  }

  next();
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical security fundamentals at junior level with real-world constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Rate limiting and lockout; constant-time comparisons; password hashing; MFA basics; secure logging/privacy.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo login flow illustrating bcrypt compare, MFA, and session issuance
async function login(req, res) {
  const { username, password, mfaToken } = req.body;
  const user = await getUser(username);
  if (!user) return res.status(401).send('Invalid credentials');
  const ok = await bcrypt.compare(password, user.hash);
  if (!ok) return res.status(401).send('Invalid credentials');
  if (user.mfaEnabled) {
    if (!mfaToken) return res.status(401).send('MFA required');
    const verified = speakeasy.totp.verify({ secret: user.mfaSecret, encoding: 'base32', token: mfaToken });
    if (!verified) return res.status(401).send('Invalid MFA');
  }
  const session = createSession(user.id);
  res.cookie('session', session, { httpOnly: true, secure: true });
  return res.send({ ok: true });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for distributed rate limiting?
- How would you test lockout and recovery flows?
- How would you store and rotate MFA seeds securely?
